[size=4][u]Station Architect[/u][/size]

This extension plans and builds your station inside the build menu. Tell it what the station should produce or build, and it works out every module the goal needs, then places them one at a time while the game's own editor snaps each one into place. Nothing is bought or built until you press the usual Apply.

[size=3][u][b]Features[/b][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] [b]Target planner[/b]: choose what the station should do, not which modules it needs. A target is a ware to produce (a number of its production modules) or ships to build or maintain (a number of build modules); several targets fit in one plan.
[*] [b]Full cycle[/b]: every intermediate ware down to the raw resources and the energy cells, with the storage to hold them. Without it, only the target's own modules and their storage.
[*] [b]Workforce[/b]: habitats for the workforce the planned modules need; [b]Self-sufficient workforce[/b] adds the food and medical supplies that workforce eats. The workforce race and the race of all other modules are separate choices; "Any" picks the best of your blueprints for each module.
[*] [b]Read from the game, not from tables[/b]: production rates with the workforce and sunlight bonuses of each production method, module sizes, connections, docking bays and approach zones, ship build times at the station's workforce. Docks and piers are sized for the station's planned traffic.
[*] [b]A preview before anything is placed[/b]: the planned modules, each ware's net per hour now and after the plan, workforce, trade volume in and out, and warnings.
[*] [b]Existing stations[/b]: the planner reads what a station already makes as its current targets, ticks Full cycle and Workforce when the station looks like it, and adds the modules for any shortfall it finds (a missing resource, habitats short of the workforce).
[*] [b]Compact layout[/b]: production, storage and habitats are packed in a tight block linked by connection modules; ship build modules sit on the sides with their approach corridors clear; docks and piers go outward.
[*] [b]A new station from the map[/b]: right-click empty space in a sector, choose [b]Plan a station here[/b], set the target, and OK hands you the game's own plot box; click where it may go, and the build menu opens with the run started.
[*] [b]Bill route[/b]: the manual alternative, a list of modules and counts from your blueprints, placed the same way.
[*] [b]Plot licence[/b]: where the plot costs money, the run shows the plot size it needs and the price, and places nothing until you buy the licence. [b]Optimize plot[/b] shrinks the plot to the planned modules afterwards.
[*] [b]The plan stays yours[/b]: move, add or remove modules by hand, save or load plans, and press Apply to pay as usual; the build menu's Cancel discards the whole unconfirmed plan.
[/list]
[size=3][u][b]Limitations[/b][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] Works only inside the open build menu of a station you own, with your own blueprints.
[*] This mod requires [b]Protected UI Mode[/b] in the game's Extensions settings to be disabled.
[*] Windows version of the game only: the placement route reads the game's own editor in memory, which the native Linux build does not expose the same way. The Windows game running on Linux through Proton or Wine should work.
[*] Verified on the 8.00 and 9.00 builds of the game. On a build it does not recognise, the panel says the editor is not reachable and Run stays off; nothing else in the build menu changes.
[*] The plot is limited to the game's 20 km: a layout that needs more is refused before anything is placed.
[*] Ship build times assume the station has its build drones; a shipyard without them builds several times slower than planned.
[*] A module that fits nowhere is reported as failed, never silently dropped.
[/list]
